:: Prologue-Marriage-Enchantment
prologueEnchantment: true
--
'But tell me,' says the old lady, her voice dropping to something almost conspiratorial, 'did you feel it—the pull? Some men speak of it as if it were destiny, others as if it were a spell. I wonder which you would call it.'

She watches him carefully. The Prince hesitates.

'There was something,' he says at last. 'Not enchantment, I think. Not the kind that... compels. Something deeper. As if she were always meant to be there, at that ball, waiting to be seen.'

The old lady nods slowly. 'There are powers in this world that are not spells, but are no less powerful for that. They are not cast—they are simply what someone is.'

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

[[What is she?|Wondering-Marriage]]  
[[What does the law say?|Wondering-Ball]]  
[[Enough of this for now|Wondering-Shoe]]  

:: Prologue-Marriage-Law
prologueLaw: true
--
'It will not be easy,' the old lady says, her expression growing grave. 'Your father's views on certain matters are... well. You know what they are.'

The Prince stiffens. 'I know what he says. I have not had the opportunity to study his reasoning.'

'The law is clear on the matter,' she continues. 'Magic—witchcraft, he calls it—is punishable by death. Not because it is evil, necessarily, but because it is unpredictable. And a kingdom cannot be ruled by unpredictability.'

The Prince says nothing. His grip tightens on the slipper.

'But laws are written by men who have never stood where you stand,' she adds, softer now. 'Who have never looked at someone and known, with absolute certainty, that they are the one. Even if that someone carries a power that the law cannot abide.'

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

[[What drew him?|Wondering-Marriage]]  
[[About the ball|Wondering-Ball]]  
[[The glass slipper|Wondering-Shoe]]  

:: Prologue-Ball
--
'It's true, you know,' the Prince says, 'that my father threw the ball in the hope that I would— that is, that it would lead to a greater acquaintance—' He stops again, rattled, and glares down at the small shoe cradled in his hands. 'I had not been very attentive to ladies in my youth.'

The old lady looks at him with an expression he cannot quite read. 'Well— my dear boy—' she begins. Then she seems to reconsider her words.

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

[[The nature of beauty|Prologue-Ball-Beauty]]  
[[The nature of memory|Prologue-Ball-Memory]]  

