:: Prologue-Ball-Beauty
prologueBeauty: true
--
'You met her in candlelight,' the old lady says carefully. 'But tell me—was it her beauty that struck you? Or was it something that her beauty revealed?'

The Prince considers this. 'They were the same thing,' he says at last. 'But not in the way you might think. It was not merely that she was beautiful. It was that her beauty seemed to... illuminate everything around her. The candles, the room, the other people. As if she were the source of light, not merely its reflection.'

'There are such people,' the old lady says. 'Born with a radiance that has nothing to do with cosmetics or dress. Nothing to do with enchantment, either. It is simply their nature.'

'Is that not a kind of magic?' the Prince asks.

The old lady is quiet for a long moment. 'It depends on what you mean by magic,' she says.

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

[[About the ball|Wondering-Ball]]  
[[About the slipper|Wondering-Marriage]]  
[[Enough of this for now|Wondering-Shoe]]  

:: Prologue-Ball-Memory
prologueMemory: true
--
'You said she left at midnight,' the old lady observes. 'But you remember her face. You remember her voice. You remember everything you said to her.'

'I do,' says the Prince. 'I wrote down some notes at the end of the evening. Everything I could remember about her, everything I recalled her saying to me. I believe I noted that she was pretty.'

'And yet you cannot recognize her now,' the old lady says. 'Even though you have seen her—perhaps—many times since.'

The Prince frowns. 'That is the trouble. I would have thought I would know. If she were someone I had seen before, I would know. But I do not. And that is what frightens me most—not the enchantment, perhaps, but the fact that I cannot be sure there was any enchantment at all.'

'There is a difference between forgetting and not knowing,' the old lady says. 'You do not forget her. You never knew her. And that is a harder thing to bear.'

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

[[About the slipper|Wondering-Shoe]]  
[[What is she?|Wondering-Marriage]]  
[[Enough of this for now|Wondering-Ball]]  

:: Prologue-Shoe
--
'I'm sure it's nothing surprising,' remarks the old lady, 'if you didn't meet anyone at the ball that you felt immediately you could marry. Such short acquaintances... there's much more to an alliance than that. One must feel at ease with the family, for instance.'

The Prince opens his mouth, then closes it again. 'I did meet someone,' he admits. 'But I know nothing of her family. It hardly matters to me, however. It will be the woman who can wear this shoe.'

He holds up the slipper. It catches the candlelight, and for a moment it seems to glow from within.

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

[[The nature of the glass|Prologue-Shoe-Nature]]  
[[The truth it holds|Prologue-Shoe-Truth]]  

