:: Prologue-Shoe-Nature
prologueNature: true
--
'Glass,' the old lady says, looking at the slipper. 'An unusual choice. Gold would have been the natural material for a royal token. Silver, even. But glass—'

She pauses, studying the Prince's face.

'Glass is transparent,' she continues. 'It shows everything. It cannot hide what is behind it. And it is fragile, easily broken.' She looks up at him. 'Do you think that is significant? That the token of your betrothal should be made of something so... honest?'

The Prince stares at the slipper. 'I never thought of it that way.'

'Perhaps you should have,' she says. 'The materials you choose to surround yourself with—they say more about you than you might imagine.'

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

[[What is she?|Wondering-Marriage]]  
[[The truth it holds|Wondering-Shoe]]  
[[Enough of this for now|Wondering-Ball]]  

:: Prologue-Shoe-Truth
prologueTruth: true
--
'You took the shoe from the stairs,' the old lady says. 'When she ran off. She left it behind, willingly or not.'

'I managed to rescue it when she ran off,' says the Prince. 'It was the only thing I had of her.'

'The shoe is glass,' the old lady says. 'It will show the truth of the foot that fits it. No deception, no alteration. Only truth.' She looks at him steadily. 'Are you prepared for what the truth might be?'

The Prince meets her gaze. 'I met someone at the ball,' he says. 'I felt for her. I thought to recognize her again by her shoe. If the shoe is what you say it is—then I am prepared for whatever it shows me.'

The old lady nods, and there is something like respect in her eyes. 'Then you are braver than your father gives you credit for.'

The humans take this in, and wait for the talk to turn once more.

