:: Wondering-Ball
[if skipPrologue]
'You skipped the small talk,' the old lady says. 'Good. It means you know what matters.'
[continue]
[unless skipPrologue]
'It wasn't a masked ball,' the old lady says. 'It wasn't even badly lit. Your father did not stint on the candles at all.'

The Prince opens his mouth.

'Oh, dear, dear, I am being odiously prying. I'm so sorry. Perhaps you met this young lady out on the terrace...' She trails off, suggesting the very faintest disapproval of young ladies who linger on terraces, out of sight of chaperones, to hold conversations with Princes, and never tell their names.

'I met her in candlelight,' says the Prince, goaded. 'But she left me at midnight.'

The effect of this comment is like the shattering of glass: for a moment you wonder if he dropped the shoe...? No. It is merely that he has admitted, or near-as-admitted, that his chosen bride is one who appeared to him under a glamor, a magical enchantment. A crime punishable by death under his father's law.

Everyone remains very very quiet.
[continue]

[if prologueBeauty]
'You said her beauty illuminated the room,' the old lady observes. 'Not that she was beautiful—that she made everything around her luminous. That is not a trick of the light, my dear boy. That is a nature, not a spell.'
[continue]
[if prologueMemory]
'You wrote down everything you could remember about her,' the old lady says. 'And yet you cannot place her. There is a difference between forgetting and never having known. Which, do you think, is your burden?'
[continue]

[[Proceed to the fitting|Fitting-Ball]]  

:: Wondering-Shoe
[if skipPrologue]
'You skipped the small talk,' the old lady says. 'Good. It means you know what matters.'
[continue]
[unless skipPrologue]
'I-- managed to rescue the shoe when she ran off,' says the Prince, a little embarrassed-looking.

'It's fortunate she left behind such a distinctive token,' says the old lady, her eyebrows raised in mischief. 'Though I wonder—do you think she intended to be found by it?'

The Prince considers this. 'I don't know. Perhaps she hoped I would find it. Perhaps she hoped I would look for her.' He looks down at the slipper. 'Or perhaps she intended for no one to find it at all.'

The old lady says nothing. She does not need to. The silence says everything.
[continue]

[if prologueNature]
'You noted the honesty of glass,' she says. 'Transparent, fragile, unable to conceal. That is why it was chosen for this purpose. Not because it is royal, but because it cannot lie.'
[continue]
[if prologueTruth]
'The shoe will show the truth of the foot that fits it,' the old lady says. 'No deception. Only truth. The question is whether you are prepared for what it might be.'
